/**
 * Compliance cluster — who pays for pest control in a Texas rental.
 *
 * THE CENTRAL HONESTY POINT OF THIS PAGE, verified Aug 2026:
 * Texas has NO statute expressly requiring landlords to provide pest control.
 * Every section of Property Code ch. 92 subchapter B was enumerated and NONE
 * mention pests, insects, vermin, rodents, bed bugs or infestation. Chapter 92
 * has no pest subchapter. The widely-circulated claim on competitor blogs that
 * "Texas law requires landlords to provide pest control" is false and this page
 * says so.
 *
 * What IS true: an infestation reaches the landlord's repair duty only if it is
 * a condition that "materially affects the physical health or safety of an
 * ordinary tenant" under §92.052(a)(3)(A) — a fact question — AND the tenant
 * follows the notice procedure AND is not delinquent in rent.
 *
 * Verified statute detail:
 *  - §92.052(a): duty arises if (1) tenant specifies the condition in a notice
 *    to the person/place rent is normally paid; (2) tenant is not delinquent in
 *    rent when notice is given; (3) the condition materially affects the
 *    physical health or safety of an ordinary tenant [or the 120°F hot water
 *    device failure].
 *  - §92.052(b): NO duty for a condition caused by the tenant, a lawful
 *    occupant, a family member, or a guest/invitee — unless normal wear and tear.
 *  - §92.052(d): notice must be in writing only if the lease is in writing and
 *    requires written notice.
 *  - §92.056(b): six conditions for landlord liability, including a SUBSEQUENT
 *    written notice after a reasonable time, OR the original sent by certified
 *    or trackable mail.
 *  - §92.056(c) VERBATIM: landlord considered to have received notice when
 *    actually received, or when USPS has ATTEMPTED delivery.
 *  - §92.056(d): rebuttable presumption that SEVEN DAYS is a reasonable time,
 *    rebuttable by date of notice, severity and nature of the condition, and
 *    availability of materials, labor and utilities.
 *  - §92.056(e): remedies — terminate; repair and deduct per §92.0561; deduct
 *    without court action per §92.0561; judicial remedies per §92.0563.
 *  - §92.056(g): the lease must state the §92.056 and §92.0561 remedies in
 *    UNDERLINED OR BOLD print.
 *  - §92.053: burden of proof.
 *
 * ⚠️ The research flagged that §92.056(b) and (e) could not be obtained fully
 * verbatim from any accessible source. This page therefore paraphrases those
 * two subsections and does NOT present them as quotations.
 *
 * Also used: H&S Code §343.011(c)(3) (unsanitary condition likely to attract or
 * harbor mosquitoes, rodents, vermin) as the code-enforcement route, and
 * §342.004 pulling it in for municipalities.
 *
 * NOT LEGAL ADVICE. The page says so twice and routes readers to a lawyer or
 * to a tenant assistance service for anything turning on their facts.
 */
